Concurrency Vs Parallelism

1. What is Concurrency?
	Concurrency means managing multiple tasks that make progress during overlapping periods of time.
	Tasks can take turns executing. They don't need to run at the exact same instant.
	Example: A web server handles many requests while waiting for database responses.
	Goal: Keep the system responsive and avoid wasting time while tasks wait for I/O.
	Common techniques:
		- Threads and thread pools
		- Async/await
		- Event loops
		- Coroutines
		- Message queues
		
2. What is Parallelism?
	Parallelism means executing multiple tasks at the exact same time, typically using multiple CPU cores, processors, machines, or GPUs.
	Example: Splitting a large dataset into chunks and processing them simultaneously.
	Goal: Finish computationally intensive work faster.
	Common applications:
		- Image and video processing
		- Machine learning
		- Data analytics
		- Compression
		- Large-scale calculations
		
3. Key Differences
	Feature			Concurrency				Parallelism
	
	Meaning			Multiple tasks make progress		Multiple tasks execute simultaneously
	Main goal		Responsiveness				Faster computation
	CPU cores		Can work on one core			Requires multiple execution units
	Best suited for		I/O-bound tasks				CPU-bound tasks
	Example			Handling 10,000 open connections	Processing data on 8 CPU cores
	Common techniques	Async I/O, threads, event loops		Multithreading, multiprocessing, GPU computing
	
	These concepts are different, although modern applications frequently use both. 
	
4. Four Possible Scenarios
	1. Neither concurrent nor parallel
		One task completes before the next starts.
	2. Concurrent, not parallel
		Tasks take turns on a single CPU core.
	3. Parallel execution
		Independent pieces of work run simultaneously on multiple cores.
	4. Concurrent and parallel
		Multiple tasks are in progress, and multiple tasks execute simultaneously across cores.
	Interview nuance: Parallelism requires independent pieces of work that can run at the same time. A single job can be divided into such pieces even if there is only one top-level job.
	
5. Real-World Examples
	Web Server
		Request A → Waiting for database
		Request B → Processing response
		Request C → Waiting for API
		Concurrency lets the server work on other requests instead of sitting idle while one request waits.
		
	Video Processing
		Frame 1 → Core 1
		Frame 2 → Core 2
		Frame 3 → Core 3
		Frame 4 → Core 4
		Parallelism lets multiple frames be processed simultaneously.
		
	E-commerce Application
		Request
		   ├── Fetch user details
		   ├── Fetch product details
		   └── Fetch delivery estimate
		These independent API calls can run concurrently. CPU-intensive calculations, if needed, can also run in parallel.
		
6. Common Mistakes
	- Concurrency is not parallelism. A single-core CPU can handle concurrent tasks without executing them simultaneously.
	- Async does not automatically mean parallel. An event loop can handle many tasks on one thread.
	- More threads don't always mean better performance. Too many threads can increase memory usage and context-switching overhead.
	- Parallelism is not always faster. Small tasks may cost more to split and coordinate than they save.
	- More concurrency can overload a database. Use connection pools, limits, timeouts, and backpressure. 
	
Concurrency is the ability to manage multiple tasks whose execution overlaps in time, while parallelism is the simultaneous execution of multiple tasks. Concurrency improves responsiveness, especially for I/O-bound workloads, whereas parallelism can speed up CPU-intensive workloads by using multiple processing units.

